## Test Anti-Pattern Audit: `ProductCatalogTests.cs`

### Summary

**10 tests analyzed · 8 Critical · 2 Medium · 0 Low**

This is a textbook **coverage-touching** suite. Every public method on `ProductCatalog` is called (achieving high line coverage), but almost nothing is actually verified. A bug in any method except a total crash would go undetected.

---

### 🔴 Critical Findings

#### 1. Coverage-Touching Pattern (systemic — 6 of 10 tests)

The suite methodically walks the entire API surface without meaningful assertions. These tests pass even if every method returns wrong results:

| Test (line) | What it does | What it proves |
|---|---|---|
| `AddProduct_Works` (L17) | Calls `AddProduct` | Nothing — no assertion at all |
| `UpdatePrice_Works` (L31) | Calls `UpdatePrice` | Nothing — price could stay 9.99 |
| `RemoveProduct_Works` (L38) | Calls `RemoveProduct` | Nothing — product could still exist |
| `GetProductCount_Works` (L63) | Calls `GetProductCount` | Nothing — count is discarded |
| `ApplyBulkDiscount_Works` (L68) | Calls `ApplyBulkDiscount` | Nothing — prices could be unchanged |
| `ImportFromCsv_Works` (L84) | Calls `ImportFromCsv` | Nothing — import could silently fail |

**Fix — add assertions that verify the observable effect:**

```csharp
// AddProduct_Works → verify the product is retrievable
[TestMethod]
public void AddProduct_StoresProduct()
{
    var product = new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m);
    _catalog.AddProduct(product);

    var stored = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");
    Assert.AreEqual("Widget", stored?.Name);
    Assert.AreEqual(9.99m, stored?.Price);
}

// UpdatePrice_Works → verify the price actually changed
[TestMethod]
public void UpdatePrice_ChangesStoredPrice()
{
    _catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m));
    _catalog.UpdatePrice("SKU-001", 14.99m);

    var updated = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");
    Assert.AreEqual(14.99m, updated?.Price);
}

// RemoveProduct_Works → verify product is gone
[TestMethod]
public void RemoveProduct_MakesProductUnretrievable()
{
    _catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m));
    _catalog.RemoveProduct("SKU-001");

    Assert.IsNull(_catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001"));
}

// GetProductCount_Works → verify actual count
[TestMethod]
public void GetProductCount_ReturnsNumberOfProducts()
{
    Assert.AreEqual(0, _catalog.GetProductCount());
